      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14254138/assets/7f36258f966a7af5ae9f35c4e3214bb8c2c7d0ae66039256b2484df5b34d83fb.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{g(3+1)-g(3-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-4}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
